invariant: INV-49
file: chacha20_avx2.c
catches: test/lint-frame-align.sh
reason: pass loses the attribute that makes clang align its frame to 32
  bytes. Its ten double rounds store and reload six state words in 32-byte
  stack slots, and clang then places those slots wherever the caller's
  stack pointer leaves them: at 6 of the 256 positions a stack takes within
  a page one slot lies across a page boundary, and the exclusive-or of 16
  KiB took up to 1.84 times as long on an EPYC 7763. Every test still
  passes, on every CPU, because the bytes are the same. gcc aligns the
  frame on its own and CI compiles with gcc, so no build of check runs the
  slower code: the lint reads what the pinned clang compiles for x86-64.

--- old
__attribute__((force_align_arg_pointer)) static lanes
--- new
static lanes
